# hi's own flags, one row each: <flag>|<needs>|<script var>|<first arg>|<help>
#
# The one place they are spelled. hi.sh reads it to dispatch (a row with a
# script var execs ${!var} with the arg first; the rest are hi.sh's own case
# arms) and to print --help; common/targets.sh reads it so `hi --<TAB>` offers
# the same list. <needs> is what a flag wants that the payload does not carry:
# `-` for nothing (works in a session), `scripts` for scripts/ (a package ships
# it, a session does not), `tests` and `git` for tests/ and .git (neither a
# package nor a session has those). docs/hi.1 is the long form and is
# drift-checked against this by tests/hi/parse_test.sh.
--help|-|||this text
--version|-|||the version: stamped at packaging time, or git describe
--packages-preview|-|||the package-priority legend, as the header prints it
--doctor|scripts|_HI_DOCTOR||[--json] [target] a read-only pre-flight report: the tree, the overlay, every backend probed, and with a target, what it resolves to; --json for a bug report
--install|scripts|_HI_INSTALL||install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files
--uninstall|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--uninstall|take those lines back out again
--configure|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--features-only|revisit the feature toggles, leaving the rc wiring be
--check-configs|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--check-configs|re-run just the shell rc syntax validation
--overlay-init|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--overlay-init|make the config overlay a git repo, in place
--color-preview|scripts|_HI_COLOR_PREVIEW||what every ssh host and your user resolve to, in color
--update|git|||git pull in the say-hi checkout (needs its .git)
--test|tests|_HI_TEST_RUN||run the test suite (needs the full checkout)
